#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2816f2b-6c4e-52d9-94f7-482b59dd7020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360711 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $f(x)=x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}$ means that the function $f$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nf:x↦x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374485 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d1/d13bfa3f5b0a9456c3251e9363c132578ba0d93b1b5a76c5b375c0c1abc7e310.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1352187-54c7-5a62-9d05-9f2859a738dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374485 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(5,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(5)^{2}+16(0)^{2}\\,>\\,64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\ge 64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\ge 64}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374486 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374486 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374486 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374486 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374486 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b79ae90c-0d36-5d1c-8416-c9af790b4f27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374487 #edb/ref 1080 "\\ge " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374487 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374487 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "296014ff-439c-53ce-b7f8-4e11aadbf4a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374488 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374488 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374488 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374488 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186374489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374488 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "36599e8f-789f-5518-8d65-81d09037ca61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374489 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374489 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374489 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d4de7c3-95e7-59a5-b6dd-15e14af73f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374490 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374490 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374490 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374490 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/